media_url (text, e.g., link to an image or video demonstration).

Changes:

Movement Relation: Introduce or update the Exercise model to include a movement_id field and ensure it establishes a foreign key relationship to the Movements table. In code, perhaps load the Movement when loading an Exercise if needed to get category or muscle info.

Name vs Variation: The name is likely the full name of the exercise as displayed to the user (e.g., “Barbell Bench Press”). The variation can store additional info like variation or customization (e.g., “Incline” or “Dumbbell”). If in prior implementation these were combined or handled differently (for instance, maybe the name included the variation), we should clarify their usage. The new approach could be to store base movement (like “Bench Press”) via movement_id and any tweak in variation (“Incline, dumbbells”) in that field. Front-end impact: When showing an exercise, consider showing Movement.name plus variation if variation is not empty. Also provide a way to choose a base movement and input a variation when creating a custom exercise.

Media URL: If not previously used, now we have a slot for an image or video link for the exercise. The UI could use this to show an illustration of the exercise. Ensure any seeding or data entry allows this field (perhaps optional). If left empty, front-end can show a placeholder or nothing. No code change is needed beyond making sure the field is accessible; just note for future feature to allow adding media.

If there was an old table for exercises without movement, a migration is needed to split them: e.g., if old design had one table of exercises with muscle info, now we have two (Movements and Exercises). We will manage Movements next:

Movements (Exercise Catalog): The public.movements table defines fundamental movements or exercise categories. Fields:

id (UUID PK),

key (text, unique code for the movement),

name (text, human-readable name),

category (ENUM movement_category, e.g., compound vs isolation),

primary_muscles and secondary_muscles (array of text, listing muscle groups targeted),

default_equipment (ENUM equipment_type, e.g., barbell, machine, bodyweight),

is_bodyweight (boolean flag),

created_at timestamp.

Changes:

If the app did not previously have a structured movement catalog, we need to create one. Likely we’ll populate this table with a list of common movements (squat, bench press, etc.) each with a unique key. The backend should provide a service to fetch available movements for exercise creation screens. Possibly, seed the DB with initial data (maybe via a SQL script or a migration).

The backend model for Movement should treat category and default_equipment as enums. Since these are user-defined Postgres types, our code or ORM needs to map them. For example, if using a Node ORM, define the column as type ENUM with those possible values. If using plain SQL, be mindful to only insert valid values (the DB will reject invalid ones). The values available (if not explicitly listed in the schema extract) should be determined (likely 'compound' vs 'isolation' for movement_category, and 'barbell', 'dumbbell', 'machine', 'bodyweight', etc., for equipment_type – the schema snippet shows default 'barbell', but the full enum values list is not shown here).

Ensure frontend uses this data: e.g., when user chooses to add a new exercise, front could present a dropdown of movements (populated via API from this table) and perhaps check a box if exercise is bodyweight (which might set is_bodyweight = true and perhaps no weight required for sets).

If older code conflated movements and exercises in one, now we must adapt: e.g., older queries for exercise names might need to join movements for the base name. If the UI expects a single exercise name, consider computing exercise.displayName = movement.name + (variation) in either the backend (when returning exercise info) or frontend.

Workout Data (Sets): The public.exercise_sets table stores the detailed log of each set performed. This is central to workout tracking. Fields include:

id (UUID PK),

session_exercise_id (foreign key linking to which exercise in which session this set belongs),

set_index (the set number in that exercise, default 1),

Various metrics for the set: weight_kg, reps, rpe (rate of perceived exertion), tempo (text notation for lift tempo), duration_sec (time under tension or interval length), distance_m (for distance-based exercises, e.g., cardio),

Computed fields: est_1rm_kg (estimated one-rep max for that set) and tonnage_kg (volume = weight × reps),

notes (text notes for the set) and performed_at timestamp (defaults to now()).

Changes & Implementation:

Model & Foreign Key: Create or update the ExerciseSet model class for this table. Ensure it has a relation to SessionExercise (likely many-to-one, as each session exercise can have multiple sets). In the ORM or query builder, you might define ExerciseSet.belongsTo(SessionExercise) or similar, with session_exercise_id as the join key.

Set Indexing: The set_index should auto-increment for each new set in a given exercise. The database default is 1, but the app logic should explicitly assign the correct number for clarity. For example, when adding a set via the UI, the backend should query how many sets already exist for that session_exercise_id and then set the next index (or the UI can keep track and send it). This ensures if a user deletes a set and adds another, the numbering remains consistent. If the old system numbered sets differently (or not at all), update it to use this field.

Weight/Reps vs Distance/Duration: Not all sets will use every field. Some exercises are weight training (using weight_kg, reps, maybe RPE, and producing tonnage and 1RM), while others might be cardio (using distance_m, duration_sec). The backend should handle these gracefully:

If weight_kg and reps are provided and >0, calculate tonnage = weight_kg * reps and store it in tonnage_kg. Also calculate est_1RM if applicable. A common formula is Epley’s formula for estimated 1RM: est_1rm = weight_kg * (1 + reps/30) (for reps >= 1). Alternatively, since we have RPE, some formulas (e.g., using RPE 10 as true max) could be used, but to keep it simple, use a standard approach. We should implement this calculation in the backend service whenever a set is recorded or updated. In code, perhaps create a utility function calculateSetMetrics(weight, reps) to return est1RM and tonnage. This way, the logic is in one place. Verify: If the old system already calculated these on the fly in the frontend, move that logic to the backend to ensure consistency (and so the values are stored centrally). The presence of these fields suggests the intent to store them in DB for later analysis.

If the exercise is cardio (e.g., running), we might ignore weight/reps and focus on distance_m and duration_sec. In such cases, est_1rm and tonnage might not apply (could be null). That’s fine – the fields are nullable. The UI should hide or disable irrelevant inputs depending on exercise type. We might classify an exercise as strength vs cardio by checking if its Movement category or equipment implies it (maybe a boolean or a field in Movement could indicate it’s an aerobic exercise). This is more of a future feature; for now, just ensure the system doesn’t require weight for every set. The code that processes a new set should be conditional: if weight_kg is present, then require reps and compute the strength metrics; if distance_m is present (and likely duration_sec), handle it as cardio. If both types of data are present (for something like circuit training with distance and weight, rare but possible), handle accordingly.

Data Validation: Add checks in the backend for these fields. For instance, reps should be a positive integer (no negative or zero reps – though zero could indicate failed attempt but probably not needed). RPE typically ranges from 1 to 10; we might enforce 6.5–10 if using RPE only for near-max sets, but since it’s optional, at least ensure the field is within a reasonable range if provided. Weight, distance, etc., should be non-negative. If any such rules were enforced on the client only before, move them server-side as well to protect data integrity.

Notes Field: The notes text in sets allows user to jot down anything about that set (e.g., “form felt good” or “used lifting straps”). Ensure the front-end provides a way to input this (perhaps a small text box under each set entry). In the backend, simply store it; maybe trim to a reasonable length if needed (the schema doesn’t impose a length, but extremely long text might be unnecessary).

Fitness Metrics: Apart from workout logging, FitSite tracks user biometric data. The public.fitness_metrics table holds the current measurements for each user. Key points:

It has a one-to-one relationship with profiles: user_id is unique and references profiles(id), meaning each user can have at most one row here.

Fields include current weight_kg, height_cm, body_fat_percent, resting_hr (heart rate), max_hr (max heart rate), and an updated_at timestamp.

Changes/Use:

If this was not present before, implement a FitnessMetrics model and service. Likely, this table is maintained as a quick lookup of the latest metrics for a user (to avoid scanning a history table for the latest entry each time). Whenever a user updates a measurement (weight, body fat, etc.), we should update this table’s record for that user and also append a new entry to the history (see next point). The unique constraint on user_id means attempts to insert a second row for the same user will fail – so the code should use UPSERT logic or check if a row exists:

On first time (new user setup), create a fitness_metrics row for the user (possibly with some fields null or defaults).

On subsequent updates, do an UPDATE rather than insert.

Backend API: Provide endpoints like GET /me/metrics to retrieve current metrics for the logged-in user (joining profiles if necessary), and PUT /me/metrics to update them. This update handler should also create a new entry in measurement_history (see below) to record the change over time.

Data model in code: We might integrate this into the Profile model (e.g., profile service can include metrics) or keep separate. Given it’s one-to-one, an easy way in an ORM is to have Profile.fitnessMetrics as a related object. Or simply query both tables when needed.

Ensure updated_at is refreshed on changes. We might consider adding a trigger in DB to auto-update it (as seen in some supabase examples), but doing it in application code is fine too (or use Postgres ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP default if available).

Measurement History: The public.measurement_history table is the log of historical measurements for each user. Each time a user measures weight, body fat, or body circumferences, a record can be added here.

Fields: user_id (FK to profiles), measure_date (date of measurement, default today), optional metrics like weight_kg, body_fat_percent, waist_cm, hips_cm, chest_cm, notes, and a created_at timestamp.

Changes/Use:

Integrate this by creating a MeasurementHistory model. On any metric update via the profile settings or a weigh-in action, insert a row here. For example, if a user updates their weight to 70kg in the app, the backend should do: INSERT INTO measurement_history(user_id, measure_date, weight_kg, ...) VALUES(me, today, 70, ...). If the user provided multiple fields at once (weight and body fat), record both. The notes field can store context (e.g., “morning fasted weight” if user wants to note it).

The frontend can use this to display progress charts. For instance, a weight-over-time graph or body fat trend. We should implement an API endpoint like GET /me/metrics/history to fetch the list (maybe with optional filters or limits). The response can be used to render charts (perhaps we’ll integrate a graph library to show weight trend).

No direct changes to old code unless previously these were somewhere else. If an older system had a single table for metrics, now we split current vs history. Make sure not to duplicate data incorrectly: the latest measurement ideally appears in both fitness_metrics (as current snapshot) and as a record in measurement_history. If the old design had just one of these, adjust accordingly (e.g., if previously they only had history and computed latest on the fly, now after refactor we store it redundantly for efficiency).

Lift Goals: The public.lift_goals table tracks strength goals for users. Each goal is typically about hitting a target weight for a certain movement.

Fields: user_id (FK to profiles), movement_id (FK to movements, the exercise movement the goal is for), and one of the target criteria:

target_1rm_kg – a one-rep max goal,

target_weight_kg with target_reps – e.g., lift X kg for Y reps,

due_date for the goal (optional deadline),

timestamps.

Changes/Use:

Create a LiftGoal model and link it with Profile (one user can have many goals). Possibly link with Movement as well for easy access to movement name.

Implement endpoints: e.g., GET /me/goals (list all current goals), POST /me/goals (create a new goal), PUT /me/goals/{id} (update or mark completed perhaps), and maybe DELETE /me/goals/{id} if user wants to remove it.

The UI should allow users to set a goal. Perhaps on an exercise detail or progress screen: “Set a goal for this lift.” The form should allow either entering a target 1RM or a target weight+reps (some users prefer “I want to bench 100kg for 5 reps” vs “I want a 1RM of 120kg”). Our schema provides fields for both; the user could fill one or the other. We need to define how to interpret them:

If target_1rm_kg is set, the goal is to reach that 1RM (likely the rep count doesn’t matter, they want their maximum strength to reach that weight).

If target_weight_kg and target_reps are set (non-null), then the goal is to do that many reps with that weight.

The app could allow one or the other. Possibly, if a user enters a target weight and reps, we leave target_1rm_kg null. If they enter a 1RM goal, we leave the other two null. Add logic to enforce this in backend (don’t accept a request that fills both types – or if both are filled, perhaps treat the 1RM as derived or ignore one).

On the backend logic side, whenever a new exercise set is logged, consider checking goals: e.g., if a user just lifted 100kg × 5 reps and they had a goal to do 100kg × 5 reps, that goal is achieved. We might want to flag or record that (could be in future an achieved_at field on lift_goals, which we can add when needed). At minimum, plan to notify the user or mark goal as achieved (the UI can check if current performance meets/exceeds goal).

If the old system had no goals feature, this is entirely new – ensure to thoroughly test this addition. If it did have something (maybe in a different format), migrate existing data. For example, if previously “goals” were just in user notes or not tracked by movement, now they are structured by movement. We may need to ask users to re-enter goals if it’s new.

Integrity Constraints & Enum Types: Note the schema uses a couple of check constraints and enums (like for gender, unit_system, season, role, etc. in profiles). We must make sure the application abides by these. Summarizing:

Gender must be one of: 'male' | 'female' | 'nonbinary' | 'prefer_not'.

Unit system: 'metric' | 'imperial'.

Season: 'reset' | 'build' | 'challenge'. (These might correspond to phases in training cycles; possibly used in UI to customize content – ensure UI and backend use these terms exactly).

Role: 'client' | 'trainer' | 'admin'.

Movement category: likely 'compound' | 'isolation' (from context, default is 'compound').

Equipment type: default 'barbell', likely among 'barbell' | 'dumbbell' | 'machine' | 'bodyweight' | ....

The backend should validate any input or default to these values; the frontend should restrict selection to these options (e.g., radio buttons or dropdowns).

If using ORMs that support enum fields, utilize them. If not, at least define constants in the code for these allowed values and use them throughout (to avoid typos).

In summary, syncing the code with the database means updating model classes, relationships, and query logic to match the above tables and fields. We have to remove any outdated structures (like an old “sessions” table or separate user table) and use these unified ones. Once models are updated, run migrations (if needed) to apply any schema changes to dev and test databases, then proceed to adjust the business logic as described next.

Backend Adjustments (API & Business Logic)

With the data model in place, we now focus on the backend application logic. The goal is to ensure all functionality works under the new all-in-one structure and to implement new features supported by the schema. Key areas include authentication integration, workout session management, profile and metrics updates, and new endpoints for goals and dashboards. We’ll go through each:

Authentication & User Management

In the all-in-one branch, authentication is likely handled within the unified system rather than an external service (if using Supabase Auth, it’s part of our stack, just not a separate microservice). We need to ensure:

Auth Middleware: The backend should have a middleware or filter that authenticates requests (e.g., checking a JWT or session cookie) and provides the auth.users ID (UUID) of the logged-in user. Since our data uses that ID everywhere (as foreign keys for profile, sessions, etc.), this is crucial. If previously, the frontend directly talked to a Supabase service, now the backend might need to do that or verify tokens. For example, if using Supabase, each request might come with an Authorization: Bearer <token> header; the backend can verify this token using the Supabase public key, then extract the sub (user ID) claim. That gives the UUID to use in our queries. We should integrate such verification in a central place (e.g., an Express.js middleware or a Django authentication class, depending on stack).

Registration Flow: If registration (sign-up) was previously handled purely by Supabase (with Magic Links etc.), we have two options:

Continue letting Supabase handle sign-ups and logins (and just use their JS client in frontend, then pass token to backend for calls). In this case, the backend doesn’t need an endpoint for register/login, it just needs to trust the token.

Or, implement custom register/login endpoints in our backend that create an auth.users record and a profiles record. Given that auth.users is a special Supabase-managed table (if indeed using Supabase), it’s easier to let Supabase handle that. So likely we’ll go with option 1: Frontend uses Supabase Auth and then uses backend for app data. We must document this: The developer should not try to create users via the backend directly (unless we have the supabase admin secret and use their Admin API).

Profile Creation: On user signup, ensure a new row in profiles is created. Supabase can automate this via database trigger or the frontend can call a cloud function. But in our all-in-one refactor, we can intercept the sign-up confirmation on backend. For example, after a user verifies email, the first time they call a protected endpoint, if no profile exists for their user ID, we create one with default values. Alternatively, use a Postgres trigger (some Supabase setups do: a trigger on auth.users insertion to create a profiles row). Check if such trigger exists; if not, implement the logic in backend.

Role & Permissions: The backend should enforce role-based access where needed. E.g., only admin role can access admin endpoints. If previously using RLS (row-level security) in Postgres for Supabase, we might continue that. But since we now have our own backend, we might not rely solely on RLS – we can enforce in code. For now, make sure to incorporate checks like:

Normal users (clients) can only access their own data (their profile, their sessions, etc.). So every endpoint that fetches or modifies user-specific data should filter by user_id = currentUserId. Because our queries are now likely done with an unrestricted service role connection (if not using RLS), we must manually filter to prevent user A from seeing user B’s data. Example: The GET workouts endpoint should do WHERE workout_sessions.user_id = <current_user> internally or derive that from a join on profiles.

Trainer or Admin roles might have broader access (perhaps a trainer can see their clients’ data, admin can see everything). We should outline how that will work and implement as needed. If not immediately needed, at least have a placeholder check that prevents misuse (e.g., allow admin to bypass the filter if we implement an admin view).

Logout: If using JWTs, “logout” is usually handled client-side by discarding the token. We might not need a server endpoint. But if the app uses refresh tokens, possibly an endpoint to revoke them. Supabase has an API for sign-out we could call. Mark this for completeness: instruct the developer to ensure the front-end properly clears credentials on logout and that any refresh token (if stored) is invalidated (Supabase’s JS library can handle that).

Workout Session & Exercise APIs

With the data model updated, implement and/or adjust the backend endpoints for managing workouts:

Create a Session (POST /workouts/sessions): This should create a new workout session record. The backend should:

Authenticate the user (ensure we have user’s UUID).

Insert into workout_sessions with user_id = currentUser, and possibly allow a title or notes from the request body (if user wants to label the session, e.g., “Leg Day” or “Monday WOD”). If none provided, title could default to something like “Workout on {date}” or remain null.

Return the created session ID and data. The front-end might then redirect to the session detail page to start adding exercises.

Add Exercise to Session (POST /workouts/sessions/{sessionId}/exercises): This creates a session_exercises entry for a given session. The request likely contains an exercise_id (or enough info to create an exercise if it’s a custom one – see below). Steps:

Verify the sessionId belongs to the current user (to avoid anyone adding to someone else’s session).

If the client already has an exercise_id (from selecting an existing exercise from library), use it. If the user is defining a new exercise on the fly (e.g., new variation not in their list), you might need to create a new entry in exercises first. We should support both. Perhaps the UI provides a list of known exercises plus an “Add new exercise” option which would call an API to create a new exercise (and possibly new movement if truly new).

For now, assume the exercise exists (the simpler flow).

Determine the next order_index for that session. You can do a quick count: SELECT count(*) FROM session_exercises WHERE session_id = {sessionId} and use that as the index (or use max(order_index)+1).

Insert the session_exercise row. Return its ID and maybe the order index to the client.

Optionally, the response could include the full exercise details (by joining exercises and movements) so the UI has everything to display immediately.

Log a Set (POST /workouts/sessions/{sessionId}/exercises/{sessionExerciseId}/sets): This will insert a new exercise_sets record.

Confirm the session and sessionExercise belong to the user (so one cannot add sets to someone else’s workout).

Read the set data from request: typically weight, reps, RPE, etc., depending on exercise type.

Determine the set_index: as discussed, likely SELECT count(*) FROM exercise_sets WHERE session_exercise_id = {sessionExerciseId} then +1. (Or the client might pass an index if it deleted something and wants to reuse numbers – but safer to recalc to avoid conflicts).

Insert the exercise_set with all the fields. Use the helper to compute tonnage_kg and est_1rm_kg before inserting (or you can insert first and update, but doing it in one step is fine). For example:

est_1rm = None
tonnage = None
if weight and reps:
    tonnage = weight * reps
    est_1rm = round(weight * (1 + reps/30), 2)  # rounding to 2 decimals perhaps
insert into exercise_sets (...) values(..., est_1rm, tonnage, ...);


Return the created set (with computed fields). The frontend can then display the 1RM calculation to the user if desired (“Est. 1RM for this set: 100 kg” etc).

Edit/Delete Set: Provide endpoints to modify or delete a set in case of mistakes:

PUT /workouts/sessions/{sessionId}/exercises/{sessionExerciseId}/sets/{setId} to edit a set’s values (weight, reps, etc.). Recalculate metrics on edit.

DELETE /workouts/sessions/{sessionId}/exercises/{sessionExerciseId}/sets/{setId} to remove a set. If we allow deletion, consider if we renumber the remaining sets’ indices or leave a gap. It might be acceptable to leave a gap (e.g., sets 1,2,4 if #3 was deleted) or we could re-sequence. Re-sequencing is cosmetic since order is inherent in creation time too. It might be simpler not to renumber past sets, to avoid confusion or concurrency issues, unless the user specifically wants a contiguous 1..N labeling. We can decide to renumber in the client display only. This is a minor detail – document whichever approach. (Perhaps for clarity, we do want to renumber so that if a user deletes set 2 of 5, the next added set becomes #5 not #6. We can accomplish by deleting and then for all sets with index > deleted, subtract 1. That’s a small loop or additional SQL update – doable because set counts per exercise are usually small. We can implement that logic in the backend delete handler.)

Complete Session (POST /workouts/sessions/{id}/complete): As mentioned, mark a workout session as finished. The endpoint simply sets ended_at = now() for that session record. It might also perform some summary calculations (not stored in DB, but maybe returned to user) like total tonnage of the session, or total duration (difference between started_at and now), etc., which the UI could show as a post-workout summary “Great job! You lifted X kg across Y sets in Z minutes.” Those can be computed on the fly by summing exercise_sets.tonnage_kg and counting sets, etc.

If the UI has a concept of “pausing” a workout (maybe the user can pause timer), we did not model that. We could ignore pause for now, or implement it purely on the client as a timer. It doesn’t affect the data except delaying when ended_at is set. If needed later, we might add a field like is_paused or track segments of active time.

Fetching Data (Dashboard): Provide endpoints to retrieve the structured workout data for display in the UI:

GET /workouts/sessions – return a list of sessions for the current user. Possibly support query params to filter by date range, etc. The list items could include summary info (like session date, title, and maybe total exercises, total sets, volume).

GET /workouts/sessions/{id} – detailed view of one session: include the exercises and sets within it. We can construct a nested JSON: e.g., session { id, date, ..., exercises: [ { exercise name, order, sets: [ {weight, reps, etc} ] }, ... ] }. This can power a detailed workout review screen.

These endpoints should leverage the relationships; e.g., join session_exercises and exercises to get exercise names, and join exercise_sets to count sets, etc. Or multiple queries/ORM calls assembling objects. Ensure to filter by user_id as always.

Consider performance: If user has lots of history, paginate the sessions list. Default to say 10 most recent, with option to get more.

Profile, Metrics, and Goals APIs

Next, ensure that profile management and the newly introduced metrics and goals are fully supported on the backend:

Profile Viewing (GET /me/profile): Returns the user’s profile information. This includes fields in profiles as well as perhaps the auth email (which is in auth.users – if we want to display email or username, we might need a join or a separate call to auth). Since auth.users might not be directly queryable without Supabase’s functions (or unless we have rights), perhaps store email in the profile as well if needed for display. But better security practice is to fetch from auth only when needed. Supabase provides auth.uid() and auth.email() in Postgres if using RLS functions. Alternatively, the backend might decode JWT which contains the email. So we can get it from the token and include it in response. Decide based on how sign-in is implemented.

Profile Update (PUT /me/profile): Allows user to edit fields like display_name, avatar_url, bio, birthdate, etc. The backend should:

Validate inputs (e.g., display_name length, bio length, birthdate is a valid date and not in future, etc.).

Ensure fields like role cannot be arbitrarily changed by a client (a normal user should not elevate to trainer/admin on their own!). Possibly ignore role changes unless the current user is an admin editing someone’s profile (which could be a different endpoint).

Update the profiles row for that user. If unit_system or season are changed, it’s fine – they have defaults and allowed sets (the constraint will reject invalid strings).

If avatar_url is provided and maybe needs processing (e.g., if user uploads an image file, we might need to first upload it to a storage (like Supabase Storage or S3) and then save the URL). That would be an integration point to consider, though outside pure refactor scope. For now, if they provide a URL or we assume they’ll use an external link, just save it.

Fitness Metrics (GET /me/metrics and PUT /me/metrics):

The GET should fetch the fitness_metrics row for the user. If none exists (shouldn’t happen if we create one at profile creation, but handle gracefully), return nulls or a not-found, so the front-end knows to prompt initial setup.

The PUT will handle updates as described: Upsert the row in fitness_metrics and insert a new measurement_history. Specifically, if a user updates any of the fields (weight, body fat, etc.), we:

Fetch the current metrics row (to compare changes if needed).

Insert a history row with measure_date = today (or user-specified date if we allow historical entry) and any fields that were provided. Perhaps fill notes if the API allows a note.

Update the fitness_metrics row’s fields to the new values and set updated_at = now().

One nuance: If the user only updates some fields (say they only entered a new weight today but not a new body fat), we should decide how to handle the other fields in history. We could carry over the last known body_fat into the history entry or leave it null. It might be better to record only what was explicitly measured. Possibly the front-end will always send the full set of current metrics when saving (so the user reviews all values). That would simplify things: treat it as a full new check-in every time. We should clarify this in the docs or UI: e.g., when updating weight, also confirm if they want to update other metrics or keep them same. For now, assume we get all fields.

Ensure these endpoints are protected and only allow the user to affect their own metrics.

Goals (GET /me/goals, POST /me/goals, etc.):

GET should retrieve all active goals for the user from lift_goals. Possibly filter out or mark those that are past due date or achieved (if we had an achieved flag, which currently we do not in schema). For now, we can consider all rows as active goals. If due_date is in the past, front-end might highlight it as overdue.

The goals data can be joined with movements to get the movement name for display (since goal references movement_id but user will want to see “Squat” or “Deadlift”).

POST to create a new goal: Validate input – must include movement_id and at least one target (either target_1rm or both target_weight & target_reps). Enforce one type:

If target_reps is given, ensure target_weight is given (and vice versa).

If target_1rm is given, ignore weight/reps if they were sent or require they not be sent.

Don’t allow zero/negative values; ensure reps is a small reasonable number (e.g., 1–20).

due_date can be optional; if provided, ensure it’s a future date (or at least today).

Insert the goal. We might also consider preventing duplicate goals for the same movement (a user could have multiple goals for one lift, but it might confuse things – for simplicity, maybe allow only one goal per movement at a time? Not mandated by schema though, which doesn’t enforce uniqueness except combination could be logically unique if desired).

In responses, format the goal nicely for UI – maybe calculate percentage progress if we have current strength info. E.g., we know user’s current 1RM (we can derive from their exercise_sets or store best lifts somewhere). That’s a potential future addition: tracking personal bests. For now, UI can compute it by scanning history or we add an endpoint GET /me/stats (see below).

No delete or put mentioned, but it’s wise to allow updating a goal (maybe to change due date or adjust target) and deleting if the user cancels that goal. Implement those as needed with similar checks.

Business logic – checking goal achievement: We should plan how the app checks if a goal is met. Since we have all workout data, we can derive the one-rep max for any set or find the heaviest weight for a given rep count a user has done. Possibly implement a helper function that given a goal, searches the exercise_sets to see if the target is satisfied:

If goal.target_1rm_kg is set: compute the user’s estimated 1RM for that movement (the highest est_1rm_kg in any set of that movement). If >= target, goal achieved.

If goal.target_weight_kg and target_reps: check if any set in exercise_sets for that movement has weight >= target_weight and reps >= target_reps. If yes, achieved.

